      "solution": "height, width = map(int, input().split())\nexpected_width = 1\nvalid = True\nhas_crown = False\nhas_trunk = False\nwidths = []\n\nfor _ in range(height):\n    row = input()\n    row_width = row.count(\"@\") + row.count(\"#\")\n    if row_width == 0:\n        continue\n    if \"#\" in row:\n        has_trunk = True\n        expected_width = 1\n    if row_width != expected_width:\n        valid = False\n    widths.append(row_width)\n    if \"@\" in row:\n        has_crown = True\n        expected_width += 2\n\nif valid and has_crown and has_trunk:\n    print(*widths)\nelse:\n    print(\"INVALID\")\n",
      "statement": "In a tree, {{@}} forms the crown and {{#}} forms the trunk. Ignore all other characters.\n\n<<Guaranteed:>> tree rows are consecutive, centered, solid blocks of {{@}} or {{#}}. Crown rows have odd widths and precede any trunk rows.\n\nA valid tree has crown rows of widths {{1}}, {{3}}, {{5}}, ... and trunk rows of width {{1}}, with at least one row of each. The crown may stop early.\n\nIf valid, print all tree row widths from top to bottom, separated by spaces. Otherwise, print {{INVALID}}.",
